<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achilles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achilles is the greatest Greek warrior of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> in Greek legend and the central figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. The son of the hero Peleus and the sea-nymph Thetis, he killed the Trojan prince Hector and was himself killed by an arrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was worshipped as a hero, notably around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> at Olbia and on the island of Leuke, and the Molossian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> claimed descent from his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achilles.jpg" alt="Achilles" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_fighting_against_Memnon_Leiden_Rijksmuseum_voor_Oudheden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
